# What do you like the most and the least about the team, company, project?
During the first part of the interview I usually try to get a feeling on at least 1 of the 3 aspects mentioned above. 
This allows me to narrow it down - ideally I get the company details first but if interviewers are happy to talk about the team instead, I'm happy with that too!
It's then easier to steer towards more detailed team/project pros and cons.

I would suggest asking about each aspect at least once, so you get both high and low level feedback.
The further away from your direct project and team, the higher level questions are more fitting. 
You probably care about product-level concerns from the CEO, but not that much if they like working with company owners that much.


## What to focus on
There are 2 main objectives I focus on here:
- Estimate what issue you may experience at your future position and what opportunities you will likely have.
- What are points of view of your future colleagues - are they looking to work on issues as they arise or follow the status quo?

For both of those you will want get at least 3 opinions from different people, this reduces personal biases. 

On the other hand, it's best to get answers from people all over the company org-chart, especially if you get to talk with Software Engineers, Product Owners and Management team members.


## Challenges

### People may avoid the part about the dislikes.
If you're concerned about this, because during the first part of the interview your interviewers are avoiding the answers to tough questions - steer further away from the "dislike" and put emphasis on "what do you like, but the least" instead. 
Also, missing bits in the good parts are also very informative. 
If the biggest nice thing is that they no longer have unpaid overtime, because next deadline is in 2 months, probably a good hint.

### At least a few answers are needed to get a feedback.
If the recruitment process is very short, you may not get enough information have a somewhat reliable picture of the company. 
If you know you will only have 45 minutes with the Engineering Manager and a Software Engineer, focus more on other questions.

### Follow-up questions
It's important to see at least 1 strong and weak side of the company after you get the answer.
If you don't get it, ask a follow-up question to get more details. 

Why is it good that the project is in modern tech stack? 
Is it an issue the interviewer encountered in the past or is it about the rest of the company current state?

Why is having a large client base is bad?
Is there a tech debt, are developers responsible for direct customers support or is that a big responsibility so it's personally stressful?

Try to dig in.

### It's an open question - it may be a time hog
If you have a lot of questions, you may not have enough time to ask all of them.
This is one of those open questions that may take a lot of time to get a good answer.
If the interviewers are on a tight schedule, focus them on the bits you're most interested in.


#### Hope this helps!
#### Good luck in your hunt for a new project!
